      "blocks": {
        "q001": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What are the \"4Cs\" of a diamond?",
            "answer": "<p>When you start looking at diamonds for an engagement ring, you'll hear the same four words again and again: cut, colour, clarity and carat. These are the standards the GIA (Gemological Institute of America) uses to describe a diamond's quality, and they're the clearest language for talking about what makes one stone different from another. Cut governs how well a diamond plays with light, colour and clarity describe how close a stone comes to perfectly colourless and inclusion-free, and carat is simply its weight. None of the four work in isolation — a diamond's beauty comes from how they balance together, not from any single number on a certificate.</p>",
            "category": "Engagement Rings"
          }
        },
        "q002": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Why is cut considered the most important of the 4Cs?",
            "answer": "<p>Cut refers to the proportions, angles and symmetry a skilled cutter gives a diamond — not its shape — and it's what decides how much brilliance, fire and scintillation you actually see when the stone catches light. A diamond can be large and perfectly colourless and still look flat and lifeless if it's poorly cut, while a diamond graded Excellent by the GIA reflects light back to your eye instead of losing it out the sides or base. That's why we treat cut as the one C worth protecting first: it's the difference between a diamond that simply sits on your hand and one that genuinely sparkles. Prioritising cut also gives you room to be more flexible on colour or clarity without losing any visible brilliance.</p>",
            "category": "Engagement Rings"
          }
        },
        "q003": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How does diamond colour grading work, and does my choice of metal matter?",
            "answer": "<p>The GIA grades diamond colour on a scale from D to Z, and what's actually being measured is the absence of colour — the closer to colourless, the rarer and more valuable the stone. D to F are the colourless grades, G to J are \"near colourless\" and offer excellent value since the difference is barely visible to the naked eye, and grades further down the scale carry a noticeable warm tint. Your setting metal genuinely changes what you see: platinum and white gold suit colourless-to-near-colourless stones best, while yellow and rose gold are naturally forgiving, making a warmer-graded diamond look bright and white against the metal. Choosing your colour grade and your metal together, rather than separately, is one of the simplest ways to get more diamond for your budget.</p>",
            "category": "Engagement Rings"
          }
        },
        "q004": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What does \"eye-clean\" mean, and do I need a flawless diamond?",
            "answer": "<p>Clarity measures a diamond's natural \"birthmarks\" — internal inclusions and surface blemishes — on a GIA scale that runs from Flawless down to Included. Very few people ever need a Flawless or Internally Flawless stone, because the goal isn't a laboratory-perfect certificate; it's a diamond that looks clean to the naked eye when it's worn and loved every day. Most VS-grade diamonds are eye-clean, and many well-selected SI1 stones are too, which is often where the best value sits. Brilliant cuts like round or oval hide small inclusions well, while step cuts such as the emerald cut have open, mirror-like facets that make inclusions more visible, so it's worth protecting clarity a little more if one of those shapes has caught your eye.</p>",
            "category": "Engagement Rings"
          }
        },
        "q005": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is a diamond's carat weight the same thing as its size?",
            "answer": "<p>Not quite — carat is a measure of weight, not size, and one carat equals 0.2 grams. Two diamonds of the same carat weight can look noticeably different depending on how they're cut and which shape they are, because a poor cut buries weight in the depth of a stone rather than spreading it where you can see it. Elongated shapes like oval, pear and marquise tend to look larger than a round brilliant of the same weight, since more of their weight sits across the visible surface. It's also worth knowing that prices jump at round \"magic\" numbers like 0.50ct or 1.00ct, so a diamond just under one of those thresholds can look practically identical while costing meaningfully less.</p>",
            "category": "Engagement Rings"
          }
        },
        "q006": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What's the difference between a diamond's \"cut\" and its \"shape\"?",
            "answer": "<p>This trips up a lot of first-time buyers, so it's worth clearing up early. Shape is the outline of the stone — round, oval, pear, emerald and so on — and it's the part you choose based on personal style. Cut is the craftsmanship underneath that outline: the angles, facets and proportions that decide how much the diamond actually sparkles, regardless of which shape you've picked. Our [gemstone cuts guide](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/blogs/guide-gemstone-cuts) walks through the different shapes side by side if you'd like to compare them.</p>",
            "category": "Engagement Rings"
          }
        },
        "q007": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Which diamond shape is the most popular for engagement rings?",
            "answer": "<p>The round brilliant remains the most requested shape, and it earns that position honestly. Its facets are the most extensively studied and refined of any diamond shape for light performance, which is why a well-cut round brilliant seems to generate its own sparkle. It's also the most versatile shape across setting styles, sitting just as naturally in a simple solitaire as it does in a halo design, which makes it a forgiving starting point if you're not sure where to begin. Our [round brilliant collection](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/collections/round-brilliant-diamond-engagement-rings) shows the range we currently have in the workshop.</p>",
            "category": "Engagement Rings"
          }
        },
        "q008": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Why do so many couples choose an oval diamond?",
            "answer": "<p>The oval cut has become one of the most sought-after shapes for engagement rings, largely because of how flattering it is on the hand — its elongated silhouette creates a slimming, refined look that a round stone doesn't quite give you. It keeps much of the brilliance you'd expect from a round brilliant while offering a distinctly different character, and because it spreads its weight across a larger surface, it tends to look bigger than a round diamond of the same carat weight. It also pairs beautifully with warmer metals like yellow gold, where the curve of the stone echoes the richness of the setting. Take a look at our [oval collection](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/collections/oval-diamond-engagement-rings), or read our [oval vs round brilliant](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/blogs/guide-gemstone-cuts/oval-vs-round-brilliant) comparison if you're torn between the two.</p>",
            "category": "Engagement Rings"
          }
        },
        "q009": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What's the difference between a brilliant cut and a step cut like the emerald cut?",
            "answer": "<p>Brilliant cuts — round, oval and cushion among them — are faceted specifically to scatter light in every direction, which is what gives them that classic, fiery sparkle. Step cuts like the emerald cut are built differently: their long, open facets run parallel to one another, creating a cleaner, more reflective \"hall of mirrors\" effect rather than scattered brilliance. That open table also means clarity matters more with a step cut, since there's nowhere for an inclusion to hide the way there is in a brilliant's busier facet pattern. If you're weighing up rectangular shapes specifically, our [radiant vs emerald](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/blogs/guide-gemstone-cuts/radiant-vs-emerald) comparison walks through the two side by side.</p>",
            "category": "Engagement Rings"
          }
        },
        "q010": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What is a solitaire engagement ring?",
            "answer": "<p>A solitaire is the simplest and most enduring engagement ring style: a single diamond, held in a setting designed to do nothing but let that stone speak for itself. Because there are no accent stones to lean on, a solitaire tends to put more scrutiny on the centre diamond's cut, colour and clarity — which is exactly why we always encourage prioritising quality over size for this style. It works beautifully across every diamond shape, from a classic round brilliant to a more architectural emerald cut. Our [solitaire collection](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/collections/solitaire-engagement-rings) shows how the same simple setting looks across different stones and metals.</p>",
            "category": "Engagement Rings"
          }
        },
        "q011": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What is a halo setting, and what's a \"hidden halo\"?",
            "answer": "<p>A halo setting surrounds the centre diamond with a ring of smaller accent stones, and the effect is more than decorative — it genuinely amplifies the light coming from the centre stone, making it appear larger than its carat weight alone would suggest. A hidden halo takes the same idea but tucks the accent diamonds beneath the girdle of the centre stone, out of sight from directly above, so the ring reads as a clean solitaire until it catches the light and reveals a flash of extra sparkle. Both are a considered way to add presence to a ring without simply buying a bigger stone. Our [halo collection](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/collections/halo-engagement-rings) shows the range of settings we work with.</p>",
            "category": "Engagement Rings"
          }
        },
        "q012": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What is a trilogy (three-stone) engagement ring?",
            "answer": "<p>A trilogy ring sets three stones side by side, traditionally read as a symbol of a couple's past, present and future together — which is part of why the style carries so much meaning for the people who choose it. The three stones don't have to match in size; a common approach is a larger centre diamond flanked by two smaller ones, though the proportions and stone choices are entirely up to you. It's a style that suits any diamond shape, and it works particularly well when the stones themselves carry some personal significance. Our [three-stone trilogy collection](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/collections/three-stone-trilogy-engagement-rings) shows a range of ways the style comes together.</p>",
            "category": "Engagement Rings"
          }
        },
        "q013": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What's the difference between platinum, white gold, yellow gold and rose gold?",
            "answer": "<p>Platinum is the most durable of the precious metals and naturally white, which means it never needs replating — when it scratches, the metal simply displaces rather than wears away, and a jeweller can polish it back to its original finish. White gold achieves a similar bright, silvery look through rhodium plating over a yellow gold base, which is beautiful but does need re-plating every so often as the coating wears with daily contact. Yellow gold has come back into real favour for its warmth and heritage feel, and it happens to be forgiving of diamonds with a warmer colour grade, since the metal's tone absorbs any hint of tint rather than highlighting it. Rose gold shares that same warm-toned forgiveness without the maintenance white gold needs, thanks to the copper alloyed into it — you can browse [yellow gold](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/collections/yellow-gold-engagement-rings), [white gold](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/collections/white-gold-engagement-rings), [rose gold](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/collections/rose-gold-engagement-rings) and [platinum](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/collections/platinum-engagement-rings) side by side.</p>",
            "category": "Engagement Rings"
          }
        },
        "q014": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Should I consider a lab-grown diamond, or is natural always better?",
            "answer": "<p>A lab-grown diamond isn't an imitation — it's chemically, physically and optically identical to a natural diamond, and the GIA grades both using the exact same 4Cs framework. The real practical difference is price: lab-grown stones typically cost meaningfully less than a natural diamond of the same grade, which means the same budget can stretch to a larger stone, a higher grade, or a more considered setting. What you don't get with lab-grown is geological rarity — natural diamonds formed over a billion years underground, and that origin story matters to some people more than others. Neither option is \"better\"; it's a genuinely personal choice, and you're welcome to explore both across our [diamond engagement rings](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/collections/diamond-engagement-rings) and [lab-grown diamond engagement rings](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/collections/lab-grown-diamond-engagement-rings) collections.</p>",
            "category": "Engagement Rings"
          }
        },
        "q015": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What does \"bespoke\" jewellery actually mean at Griffin & Co?",
            "answer": "<p>\"Bespoke\" means your piece is designed and made from scratch, built around a conversation rather than picked off a shelf. That's different to \"customising\" a ring, where you might choose a standard setting and add a separate diamond. With a truly bespoke commission, we sketch your ideas, source gemstones to match your vision, and build the piece around your exact style and budget from the very first meeting. It's a genuinely collaborative process — you're involved at every stage, not just at collection. Explore our [bespoke design process](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/pages/bespoke-jewellery-designs) to see how it works from consultation to finished piece.</p>",
            "category": "Bespoke Design & Custom Commissions"
          }
        },
        "q016": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What's the first step in starting a bespoke commission?",
            "answer": "<p>Every bespoke journey begins with a complimentary, no-obligation design consultation at our Chichester showroom, or via a home visit if that suits you better. This is a relaxed conversation where we get to know you and your story — bring a Pinterest board, a family ring, a photo, or just an idea in your head, and we'll start sketching possibilities together. We'll talk through style, budget, and gemstones, and explain the whole process so there are no surprises later. You can [schedule an appointment](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/pages/schedule-an-appointment) online whenever you're ready to start.</p>",
            "category": "Bespoke Design & Custom Commissions"
          }
        },
        "q017": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "I don't have a design in mind — can I still go bespoke?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes, absolutely — many of our clients arrive with nothing more than a feeling or a few loose ideas, and that's exactly where we like to start. Our designers are there to listen, ask the right questions, and guide you through styles, stones, and metals until you recognise what you love. We'll sketch live during your consultation, so you can see your ideas take shape rather than trying to describe them from scratch. If you'd rather have a starting point first, our [online design studio](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/pages/custom-jewellery-builder-online) is also a good way to explore options before booking.</p>",
            "category": "Bespoke Design & Custom Commissions"
          }
        },
        "q018": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What happens during a bespoke design consultation?",
            "answer": "<p>Your first consultation is a relaxed, one-on-one session with one of our designers, usually lasting around an hour. We'll discuss your inspiration, style, and budget, look at examples from our collection, and browse a curated selection of diamonds or gemstones together. For bespoke pieces, this is also the first step of our CAD design process, where hand-drawn sketches and early concepts begin to take shape. There's no pressure to decide anything on the day — it's simply a chance to explore what's possible.</p>",
            "category": "Bespoke Design & Custom Commissions"
          }
        },
        "q019": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What should I bring to my consultation, and is there any obligation to buy?",
            "answer": "<p>The most useful thing you can bring is your imagination — inspirational images from Pinterest, magazines, or old photographs all help us understand what you're picturing. It's also worth having a rough idea of your budget, since that helps us make sensible recommendations from the start rather than showing you options that don't fit. Our consultations are genuinely no-obligation, and we want you to leave feeling informed and excited, not pressured into a decision. Take whatever time you need — you can always [book a consultation](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/pages/schedule-an-appointment) again once you're ready.</p>",
            "category": "Bespoke Design & Custom Commissions"
          }
        },
        "q020": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How long does a bespoke commission take?",
            "answer": "<p>Timelines vary with the type of commission, so it's worth being specific rather than quoting one number. A ring built through our [online 3D design studio](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/pages/custom-jewellery-builder-online) typically takes 4-6 weeks from order to completion, and a fully bespoke engagement ring generally follows a similar 4-6 week window from approved design to collection — though more elaborate designs or harder-to-source stones can extend a fully from-scratch commission closer to 12 weeks. A custom signet ring, with its hand or laser engraving, usually takes 4 to 8 weeks from design approval to delivery. Because complexity and gemstone sourcing both affect the timeline, we recommend starting a bespoke engagement ring at least 3 months before you plan to propose, and we'll give you a specific estimate once we understand your design.</p>",
            "category": "Bespoke Design & Custom Commissions"
          }
        },
        "q021": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What's the difference between the online design studio and full bespoke design?",
            "answer": "<p>Our [online design studio](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/pages/custom-jewellery-builder-online) lets you build a ring, band, or piece of jewellery from thousands of combinations of setting, metal, and GIA-certified diamond, and it's a great way to design at your own pace from home. If you have a completely unique vision that doesn't fit within those combinations, our full [bespoke design service](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/pages/bespoke-jewellery-designs) is the better route — you'll work from a blank canvas with our designers in person or via a home visit. Both paths lead to the same standard of UK craftsmanship and ethical sourcing; it really comes down to whether you're refining an existing idea or starting from nothing at all. You're welcome to try the online studio first and switch to a full consultation if you want more freedom.</p>",
            "category": "Bespoke Design & Custom Commissions"
          }
        },
        "q022": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What happens after I place an order through the online design studio?",
            "answer": "<p>Once you finalise your design and place your order, our master goldsmiths in Chichester review the specifications by hand before any work begins. From there, we source your chosen diamond, cast the precious metal, and hand-finish the piece in our workshop. The entire crafting process typically takes 4-6 weeks, and it's the same careful, hands-on process as an in-showroom commission — just started from your own screen.</p>",
            "category": "Bespoke Design & Custom Commissions"
          }
        },
        "q023": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How accurate are the 3D CAD renders of my design?",
            "answer": "<p>Our 3D technology produces highly accurate, photorealistic renders that are proportioned to your exact chosen diamond and ring size, so what you approve on screen is a genuine preview of the finished piece. For fully bespoke commissions, we can also produce a wax or resin prototype at full scale, letting you check fit, proportions, and comfort before we commit to metal. Between the CAD render and the prototype stage, you have real opportunities to fine-tune claw style, band width, and other details until it's right. Nothing goes to casting until you're happy with how it looks and feels.</p>",
            "category": "Bespoke Design & Custom Commissions"
          }
        },
        "q024": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What are the 4Cs, and how do they matter for a bespoke diamond?",
            "answer": "<p>The 4Cs — cut, colour, clarity, and carat — are the standard used to describe a diamond's quality, and understanding them helps you get the most from your budget on a bespoke stone. Cut affects how much a diamond sparkles, colour is graded on the GIA scale from D to Z, clarity looks at natural inclusions most of which are invisible to the naked eye, and carat is simply weight rather than visual size. A well-cut, eye-clean stone in the VS or SI clarity range often looks every bit as brilliant as a flawless one, at a more sensible price. During your consultation we'll talk through these trade-offs together — our [guide to bespoke engagement ring design](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/blogs/guide-bespoke-engagement-ring-design) covers each of the 4Cs in more depth if you'd like to read further.</p>",
            "category": "Bespoke Design & Custom Commissions"
          }
        },
        "q025": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Should I choose a natural or lab-grown diamond for my bespoke piece?",
            "answer": "<p>Both are genuine options for a bespoke piece, and which suits you depends on what matters most to you. Lab-grown diamonds are chemically identical to mined stones and typically cost around 20-40% less, with a smaller environmental footprint, while natural diamonds tend to hold their resale value more strongly over time. Whichever you choose, every diamond we work with — natural or lab-grown — is GIA-certified and hand-inspected before it goes into your setting. We're happy to show you both side by side during your consultation so you can decide with confidence.</p>",
            "category": "Bespoke Design & Custom Commissions"
          }
        },
        "q026": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What factors affect the cost of a bespoke commission?",
            "answer": "<p>The cost of a bespoke piece comes down to three main factors: the metal and gemstones you choose, how intricate the design is, and the hours of skilled handwork involved. A higher-grade diamond or a rarer coloured gemstone carries a higher cost, and detailed techniques like micro-pavé setting or multi-metal inlay take longer to execute than a simple solitaire. We give clear, itemised estimates that break down metal weight, stone cost, and finishing charges, so you can see exactly where your budget is going. If it helps, we can also discuss phased payment options during your consultation.</p>",
            "category": "Bespoke Design & Custom Commissions"
          }
        },
        "q027": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can you remodel my old or inherited jewellery into something new?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes — remodelling is one of our specialities, and it's a meaningful way to carry a piece's history forward while giving it new life. We can melt down the gold or platinum from an old ring or brooch and reset its stones into a completely new design that still carries all the original sentimental value. Clients have turned a grandmother's ring stones into a pendant, or a worn wedding band into an anniversary bangle — the possibilities depend on the condition of what you bring in. Bring your piece along to a [consultation](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/pages/schedule-an-appointment) and we'll talk through what's possible.</p>",
            "category": "Bespoke Design & Custom Commissions"
          }
        },
        "q028": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What kinds of jewellery can I have made bespoke, beyond engagement rings?",
            "answer": "<p>Our bespoke service covers far more than engagement rings. We regularly design necklaces and pendants, bracelets and bangles, earrings, cufflinks and men's jewellery, and full heirloom recreations, alongside engagement rings and wedding bands. If you have an idea — however small — we can sit down, sketch it out, and bring it to life in precious metal and gemstones. Take a look at our [bespoke jewellery designs](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/pages/bespoke-jewellery-designs) page for examples of what our Chichester workshop can create.</p>",
            "category": "Bespoke Design & Custom Commissions"
          }
        },
        "q029": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can I design a custom signet ring with my family crest?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes — custom signet rings with a family crest are one of our specialities, and we offer 9ct and 18ct yellow, white, and rose gold, as well as platinum and sterling silver depending on your preference. If you don't have a clear image of your crest, our designers can often help research or reconstruct it from fragmentary information, or create a new personal symbol that reflects your identity instead. We use traditional hand-engraving for depth and texture, or laser engraving for intricate logos and text, and our reverse-engraving technique means your ring can also be used for wax sealing. Learn more on our [custom signet rings](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/pages/custom-signet-rings-with-family-crests) page.</p>",
            "category": "Bespoke Design & Custom Commissions"
          }
        },
        "q030": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What is the Kimberley Process, and how does it apply to my diamond?",
            "answer": "<p>The Kimberley Process is an international certification scheme that prevents rough diamonds from funding armed conflict, requiring government-issued certificates that guarantee a diamond's origin and enforcing checks at borders. It's an important industry safeguard, but its state-level framework can leave gaps in traceability, which is why we go further with our own suppliers. We work with partners who provide laser-inscribed serial codes and chain-of-custody reports tracking a diamond from mine through cutting and polishing to final setting, verified by independent gem labs. If conflict-free sourcing matters to you, we're happy to talk through exactly where your stone has come from — or read more in our [ethical sourcing guide](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/blogs/guide-bespoke-jewellery-design-service/ethical-sourcing).</p>",
            "category": "Bespoke Design & Custom Commissions"
          }
        },
        "q031": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What's the difference between recycled gold and Fairmined gold?",
            "answer": "<p>Recycled gold is metal reclaimed from old jewellery, industrial offcuts, and electronic waste, then melted down and re-refined to the same purity as freshly mined gold — identical in quality, but avoiding further mining. Fairmined gold, by contrast, is newly mined but certified to come from artisanal and small-scale mining communities that meet strict standards for safety, fair pay, and environmental protection. Both are ethical choices with different focuses: recycled gold is about circularity and reducing extraction, while Fairmined is about supporting the miners and communities behind newly mined metal. We can work with either — or a mix — depending on what feels right for your piece.</p>",
            "category": "Bespoke Design & Custom Commissions"
          }
        },
        "q032": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How does Griffin & Co approach ethical sourcing in practice?",
            "answer": "<p>In practice, this means every material we use is traced from its origin to your finished piece, and we work with certified suppliers rather than taking sourcing on trust. Our metals are recycled, Fairmined, or Fairtrade certified, and our diamonds are Kimberley Process compliant, with additional chain-of-custody documentation beyond the industry minimum. It's not something we make a big show of — it's simply how every commission is built, whether you ask about it or not. If you'd like the details on a specific piece, just ask us during your consultation and we'll walk you through exactly where everything came from.</p>",
            "category": "Bespoke Design & Custom Commissions"
          }
        },
        "q033": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What gemstones does Griffin & Co offer beyond diamonds?",
            "answer": "<p>We stock eight coloured gemstones across our jewellery: sapphire, emerald, opal, amethyst, ruby, tourmaline, topaz and pearl, each with its own dedicated online collection. You'll find them set into rings, earrings, necklaces and bracelets, from single-stone pendants to full eternity bands. Browse the full range on our [by-gemstone hub](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/collections/by-gemstone), or head straight to the collection for the stone you love. If you're after something more personal, we can also source and set gemstones as part of a [bespoke commission](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/pages/bespoke-jewellery-designs).</p>",
            "category": "Gemstone Collections"
          }
        },
        "q034": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How durable is sapphire for everyday wear?",
            "answer": "<p>Sapphire rates a 9 on the Mohs hardness scale, the standard gemologists use to compare how resistant a mineral is to scratching, and second only to diamond at 10. That makes it one of the toughest gemstones you can wear day to day, which is part of why it's such a popular choice for an engagement ring or a piece you want to wear without a second thought. Our [sapphire jewellery collection](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/collections/sapphire-jewellery) includes both the classic deep blue and softer pink tones, set in gold and platinum. Because it wears so well, sapphire suits anyone who wants real colour without extra worry about everyday knocks.</p>",
            "category": "Gemstone Collections"
          }
        },
        "q035": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What makes emerald different to care for than sapphire or ruby?",
            "answer": "<p>Emerald is a form of beryl and typically rates 7.5 to 8 on the Mohs hardness scale, but that number doesn't tell the whole story. Most emeralds naturally form with tiny internal inclusions, sometimes called a \"jardin\" or garden, which can leave the stone more prone to chipping than its hardness alone suggests. We'd always recommend taking emerald rings off before anything physical and steering clear of ultrasonic cleaners, much the same care you'd give any delicate heirloom. You can see how we use it across cocktail rings, wishbone bands and floral pendants in our [emerald jewellery collection](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/collections/emerald-jewellery).</p>",
            "category": "Gemstone Collections"
          }
        },
        "q036": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Why is opal considered a more delicate gemstone?",
            "answer": "<p>Opal is softer than most other gemstones we work with, rating around 5.5 to 6.5 on the Mohs hardness scale, and it actually holds a small amount of water within its structure. That means it can be more sensitive to extreme heat and very dry conditions than a sapphire or ruby, and it isn't a stone we'd put through an ultrasonic cleaner. None of this makes opal any less special — its shifting play of colour is genuinely unlike anything else — it simply asks for a little more thought about when and how you wear it. It's the traditional October birthstone, and you'll find it in our [opal jewellery collection](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/collections/opal-jewellery).</p>",
            "category": "Gemstone Collections"
          }
        },
        "q037": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is amethyst a good everyday gemstone?",
            "answer": "<p>Amethyst is a variety of quartz, which puts it at 7 on the Mohs hardness scale, comfortably hard-wearing enough for a ring you put on most days. Its purple colour comes from trace iron within the crystal structure, ranging from a soft lilac through to a deep, almost velvety violet. It's the traditional birthstone for February, which makes it a popular choice for birthday gifts as well as everyday jewellery. Our [amethyst jewellery collection](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/collections/amethyst-jewellery) includes rings, pendants and earring sets, some pairing amethyst with diamonds for extra sparkle.</p>",
            "category": "Gemstone Collections"
          }
        },
        "q038": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What's the difference between ruby and sapphire?",
            "answer": "<p>Ruby and sapphire are actually the same mineral, corundum, which is why they share the same hardness of 9 on the Mohs scale. What separates them is colour: traces of chromium give ruby its rich red, while other trace elements produce sapphire's blues, pinks and other shades. Ruby is the traditional birthstone for July, and the combination of vivid colour and everyday durability has made it a long-standing favourite for rings. You can browse our current pieces in the [ruby jewellery collection](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/collections/ruby-jewellery).</p>",
            "category": "Gemstone Collections"
          }
        },
        "q039": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What makes tourmaline distinctive as a gemstone?",
            "answer": "<p>Tourmaline has one of the widest colour ranges of any gemstone, spanning pink, green and blue-green, with some stones shifting from one shade to another within a single crystal. It sits at 7 to 7.5 on the Mohs hardness scale, which makes it practical for rings as well as earrings and pendants. Pink tourmaline in particular has become a popular alternative to a traditional diamond centre stone, including in bespoke engagement ring designs we've created for clients. Take a look at our [tourmaline jewellery collection](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/collections/tourmaline-jewellery) to see the range of colour and cut we currently stock.</p>",
            "category": "Gemstone Collections"
          }
        },
        "q040": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What should I know about topaz?",
            "answer": "<p>Topaz rates an 8 on the Mohs hardness scale, a durable, everyday-friendly gemstone that sits between quartz-based stones like amethyst and the toughest stones like sapphire and ruby. It occurs naturally in a range of colours, though the blue tones most people associate with topaz jewellery are especially prized for their clarity and depth. It's the traditional birthstone for November, making it a natural gift choice for anyone born that month. Our [topaz jewellery collection](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/collections/topaz-jewellery) features it set across rings, earrings and pendants.</p>",
            "category": "Gemstone Collections"
          }
        },
        "q041": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How is caring for pearls different from caring for other gemstones?",
            "answer": "<p>Pearls are organic gems, formed inside molluscs rather than crystallising underground like a sapphire or amethyst, and that changes how you need to look after them. They sit at only around 2.5 to 4.5 on the Mohs hardness scale, softer than almost every other stone we work with, and their surface can be dulled by perfume, hairspray and everyday chemicals. We'd always suggest putting pearl jewellery on last, after perfume and make-up, and giving it a gentle wipe after wearing. Explore our [pearl jewellery collection](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/collections/pearl-jewellery) for necklaces, earrings and rings that make the most of their natural lustre.</p>",
            "category": "Gemstone Collections"
          }
        },
        "q042": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Should I choose a coloured gemstone or a diamond for my engagement ring?",
            "answer": "<p>There's no wrong answer here — it comes down to the durability, colour and story you want your ring to carry. Diamond remains the hardest natural mineral at 10 on the Mohs scale, so it's the safest choice if you want a stone that resists scratching under any circumstances, while sapphire and ruby offer nearly the same durability with striking colour instead. Softer stones like emerald, opal and tourmaline reward a little extra care but bring a character a diamond can't match. We stock a dedicated range of [gemstone engagement rings](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/collections/gemstone-engagement-rings) if you'd like to see how coloured stones look as a centrepiece, and we're always happy to talk through which stone suits how you'll actually wear the ring day to day.</p>",
            "category": "Gemstone Collections"
          }
        },
        "q043": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How do I choose the right shape or cut for a coloured gemstone?",
            "answer": "<p>Shape is one of the most personal choices you'll make, whether you're drawn to a classic round brilliant, an elongated oval, or something with more presence like a cushion or pear cut. Our [gemstone cuts guide](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/blogs/guide-gemstone-cuts) walks through the most popular shapes side by side, comparing how they wear on the hand and catch the light — it was written with diamonds in mind, but the same shape comparisons are a useful starting point for a coloured stone too. Coloured gemstones are sometimes cut a little differently to diamonds, since the priority can be showing off the stone's colour rather than maximising sparkle alone. As always, the best way to decide is to try a few shapes on in person at our Chichester showroom.</p>",
            "category": "Gemstone Collections"
          }
        },
        "q044": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can I have a bespoke piece made with a specific gemstone?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes — gemstone selection is part of our bespoke design process from the very first consultation. Whether you have a particular stone in mind, a birthstone, a family heirloom, or simply a colour you're drawn to, our team can talk you through the options and source stones to match your design. Bespoke pieces let you combine a coloured gemstone with the metal, setting and shape that's right for you, rather than choosing from what's already in stock. Get in touch to start the conversation on our [bespoke jewellery page](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/pages/bespoke-jewellery-designs).</p>",
            "category": "Gemstone Collections"
          }
        },
        "q045": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What are the traditional birthstones for the gemstones Griffin & Co stocks?",
            "answer": "<p>Traditional birthstone charts assign a stone to each month, and several of ours line up: amethyst for February, emerald for May, pearl for June, ruby for July, sapphire for September, opal and tourmaline for October, and topaz for November. These associations come from long-standing jewellery tradition rather than any single official body, so you'll sometimes see slight variation between different charts. They're a lovely, personal way to choose a gift, whether that's for a birthday, an anniversary, or just because. You can browse any of these stones by name in our [gemstone collections](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/collections/by-gemstone).</p>",
            "category": "Gemstone Collections"
          }
        }
      },
