    "main2": {
      "type": "faq-knowledge-base",
      "blocks": {
        "q046": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Do you create bespoke shaped or contour wedding bands to match my engagement ring?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes, this is one of our specialities. If your engagement ring has an unusual shape or a low setting, our master goldsmiths can craft a custom contour band that sits perfectly flush against it, so the two rings wear as one seamless pair. We consider how your wedding band and engagement ring will sit together at every stage of the design, checking for gaps, catches, or pressure points against the existing setting. This kind of fitted work is best discussed in person, whether at our [bespoke design studio](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/pages/bespoke-jewellery-designs) in Chichester or during a private consultation.</p>",
            "category": "Wedding Rings"
          }
        },
        "q047": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How far in advance should we order our wedding rings?",
            "answer": "<p>For bespoke designs and shaped contour bands, we recommend starting the process two to three months before your wedding date. That gives enough time for CAD design approvals, casting, and any final size adjustments before the big day. If your rings involve more detailed stone-setting or engraving, it's worth building in a little extra time so nothing feels rushed. Get in touch early and [book a consultation](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/pages/schedule-an-appointment) so we can plan around your date.</p>",
            "category": "Wedding Rings"
          }
        },
        "q048": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can we design our wedding rings online if we don't live near Chichester?",
            "answer": "<p>Absolutely — our online design studio is available to clients everywhere, so location is never a barrier to a bespoke wedding band. You can fully customise your rings from home, and if you'd like extra guidance, you can request a home visit or a video consultation with one of our goldsmiths. This works well for couples further afield across Surrey, Hampshire, and West Sussex who still want the same considered, unhurried process. Start by exploring our [Wedding Rings &amp; Bespoke Bands](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/pages/wedding-rings-bespoke-bands) page, or book an appointment when you're ready to talk it through.</p>",
            "category": "Wedding Rings"
          }
        },
        "q049": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Do our wedding rings have to match?",
            "answer": "<p>Not at all. While some couples love the tradition of matching bands, we encourage you to choose rings that reflect your individual style and comfort — think of them as a pair, not necessarily twins. You can still create a sense of togetherness by choosing the same metal or a similar finish, even if the width or setting differs between the two rings. Browse our [wedding rings collection](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/collections/wedding-rings) for ideas, or talk it through with us at a joint consultation.</p>",
            "category": "Wedding Rings"
          }
        },
        "q050": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Which metal is best for a wedding band?",
            "answer": "<p>The \"best\" metal really depends on your lifestyle and preference. Platinum is the most durable option and naturally hypoallergenic, so it suits anyone with sensitive skin or an active daily routine, while 18ct gold — in yellow, white, or rose — is the classic choice, prized for its rich colour and depth. We're happy to talk through the pros and cons of each during your consultation. You can also browse our [platinum](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/collections/platinum-wedding-rings), [yellow gold](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/collections/yellow-gold-wedding-rings), [white gold](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/collections/white-gold-wedding-rings), and [rose gold](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/collections/rose-gold-wedding-rings) collections to see how each looks.</p>",
            "category": "Wedding Rings"
          }
        },
        "q051": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What wedding band styles does Griffin & Co offer?",
            "answer": "<p>Our collection spans four main styles: classic wedding bands, for pure, timeless elegance; diamond set bands, for a subtle, brilliant sparkle; shaped and contour bands, designed to sit flush against an engagement ring; and eternity rings, symbolising enduring, unbroken love. Every style is available across our full range of metals, so you can match the look to what already suits your hand. If nothing in the ready-to-wear range feels quite right, our goldsmiths can also build a bespoke version around any of these styles. Start browsing our [classic wedding bands](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/collections/classic-wedding-bands) or [diamond set bands](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/collections/diamond-set-bands) to get a feel for what's possible.</p>",
            "category": "Wedding Rings"
          }
        },
        "q052": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What should I think about when choosing a men's wedding ring?",
            "answer": "<p>Profile and width are the two decisions that shape how a ring actually feels day to day. Most men find a court profile the most comfortable to begin with, thanks to its gently curved interior, while a flat court or D-shape profile gives a more architectural look for those who prefer it. Width typically runs from 4mm to 8mm, with 6mm widely regarded as the balanced midpoint that suits the broadest range of hand sizes — broader or longer fingers generally carry a wider band well, while slimmer fingers are often better served by 4mm or 5mm. Trying different widths and profiles in person is the most reliable way to find what's right for you. Our [men's wedding rings guide](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/blogs/jewellery-guides/mens-wedding-rings) covers this in more detail, or you can browse our [men's wedding rings](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/collections/mens-wedding-rings) collection directly.</p>",
            "category": "Wedding Rings"
          }
        },
        "q053": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How much does a wedding band typically cost?",
            "answer": "<p>Prices vary mainly with metal, width, and finish. As a general guide to current UK fine jewellery pricing, a 4–6mm 9ct gold court band typically starts in the region of £200–£400, an 18ct gold band in a standard profile generally runs from £500–£1,200, and a platinum band typically begins around £900, reaching £2,000 or more for wider, more substantial pieces. Diamond-set and fully bespoke bands are priced individually once we know your stone quality, setting complexity, and metal choice, and we'll always provide a detailed, itemised quote before any work begins. Browse our [wedding rings collection](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/collections/wedding-rings) to see current pricing on ready-to-wear pieces, or book a consultation for a bespoke quote.</p>",
            "category": "Wedding Rings"
          }
        },
        "q054": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Should we choose a ready-made wedding band or go bespoke?",
            "answer": "<p>Both are genuinely good options, and the right choice depends on your situation rather than one being better than the other. Ready-made bands suit you well if you have a clear sense of your preferred metal, width and finish, and a confirmed ring size — you can try one on and know within moments whether it's right. A bespoke commission becomes the stronger choice if your ring size falls outside standard stocked ranges, if you want your ring to deliberately echo your partner's, or if you want to incorporate an heirloom stone or inherited metal into something new. If you're weighing up bespoke, it's worth building in four to eight weeks from consultation to completion when planning around your wedding date. Explore our [bespoke wedding ring design service](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/pages/bespoke-jewellery-designs) or read more in our [guide to commissioning a custom wedding ring](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/blogs/guide-bespoke-jewellery-design-service/wedding-ring-custom-made).</p>",
            "category": "Wedding Rings"
          }
        },
        "q055": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What jewellery repair services does Griffin & Co offer?",
            "answer": "<p>Our master goldsmiths work on almost everything that can go wrong with a well-loved piece: ring resizing, claw retipping and full reclawing, chain and bracelet repair, shank replacement, stone tightening and re-setting, rhodium plating, refinishing, polishing, and engraving. Every repair is carried out in our own on-site workshop at 64 South Street, Chichester, so your piece never leaves the building or goes to a third party. We treat everyday accessories and sentimental heirlooms with the same care, whether you need a simple chain solder or a full antique restoration. Have a look at our [repair service](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/pages/jewellery-repair-service) page for the full range, or bring your piece in for a free assessment.</p>",
            "category": "Jewellery Repair & Remodelling"
          }
        },
        "q056": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How much does ring resizing cost?",
            "answer": "<p>Resizing prices depend on the metal and whether we're sizing up or down. Our published starting prices run from around £30 for a straightforward sterling silver resize up to £120 or more for platinum, with 9ct and 18ct gold sitting in between. These are indicative starting figures for plain bands; rings wider than about 4mm, or set with stones, engraving, or milgrain detailing, need an individual assessment before we can confirm a price. We always give you a firm, no-obligation quote after examining your ring in person, either at our Chichester showroom or during a private home visit. See our [repair price list](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/pages/repair-price-list) for more detail.</p>",
            "category": "Jewellery Repair & Remodelling"
          }
        },
        "q057": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Are there rings I can't have resized?",
            "answer": "<p>Most rings can be resized, but a few designs need extra thought. Eternity rings fully encrusted with stones, and rings with tension-set stones, can be structurally tricky to alter without compromising the setting. In those cases, we'll talk you through the alternatives, including ring guards or a partial resize, so you can still get a comfortable, secure fit. Bring your ring in and one of our goldsmiths will assess it properly before recommending the best approach. Learn more on our [repair service](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/pages/jewellery-repair-service) page.</p>",
            "category": "Jewellery Repair & Remodelling"
          }
        },
        "q058": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How long does a jewellery repair take?",
            "answer": "<p>It depends entirely on what your piece needs. A simple chain solder or straightforward ring resize can often be turned around within a few working days, while more involved work, such as shank replacement, full reclawing, or a repair needing stone removal, typically takes longer. Our workshop's standard turnaround for most repairs is 14 to 21 working days from drop-off, covering assessment, the repair itself, quality checking, and a final clean and polish. If you have a deadline, such as a wedding or anniversary, tell us when you book and we'll do our best to prioritise your piece. Visit our [repair service](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/pages/jewellery-repair-service) page to get started.</p>",
            "category": "Jewellery Repair & Remodelling"
          }
        },
        "q059": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is there a minimum charge for repairs?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes. Because of the time, skill, and equipment involved, we have a minimum charge of £30 for our repair services. That covers the specialist's time, minor materials, and use of professional equipment, and we'll always give you a transparent quote before any work begins, so there are no surprises. Bring your piece into our Chichester showroom, or get in touch to arrange an assessment. No appointment is necessary for straightforward repairs.</p>",
            "category": "Jewellery Repair & Remodelling"
          }
        },
        "q060": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What does claw repair or retipping involve, and what does it cost?",
            "answer": "<p>The claws that hold your stones in place gradually wear thin with everyday contact, and worn claws are one of the most common ways a stone gets lost. Retipping rebuilds the worn prong with new metal to restore height and grip, re-securing the stone before any damage occurs. Starting prices run from around £25 per claw in sterling silver up to £55 or more per claw in platinum, with a full four-claw reclaw on a classic solitaire typically falling between £120 and £180 or more depending on metal and complexity. If you ever feel the faintest rattle when a stone moves, it's worth having it looked at promptly. Book a free assessment via our [repair service](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/pages/jewellery-repair-service) page.</p>",
            "category": "Jewellery Repair & Remodelling"
          }
        },
        "q061": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can you repair a broken chain, clasp, or bracelet?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes, this is one of our most frequent repairs. We solder broken links, replace worn or snapped clasps, and restore damaged sections of necklaces, bracelets, and anklets so you can wear them with confidence again. Starting prices run from around £18 for a simple jump ring solder up to £60 or more for a bracelet repair in 18ct gold or platinum, and every repair in this category comes back to you with a complimentary clean and light polish. We always use carat-matched solder, so a 9ct chain is repaired with 9ct solder and an 18ct chain with 18ct, preserving the hallmark. Get in touch through our [repair service](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/pages/jewellery-repair-service) page for a quote.</p>",
            "category": "Jewellery Repair & Remodelling"
          }
        },
        "q062": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What is rhodium plating, and when do I need it?",
            "answer": "<p>White gold's crisp, bright-white finish comes from a thin layer of rhodium plated over the metal's naturally warmer tone. That plating wears with daily contact, particularly on ring shanks, so re-plating every 12 to 24 months is a normal part of owning white gold jewellery rather than a sign anything has gone wrong. If your white gold ring has started to look a little yellow or uneven, that's your cue. Starting prices run from around £50 for a sterling silver or 9ct white gold ring up to £90 for more intricate pieces, and every service includes a thorough pre-plate clean and polish. Book a visit through our [repair service](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/pages/jewellery-repair-service) page.</p>",
            "category": "Jewellery Repair & Remodelling"
          }
        },
        "q063": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "My ring doesn't sparkle like it used to. Does it need a repair?",
            "answer": "<p>Not necessarily. This is one of the most common things we see, and it's usually caused by everyday build-up from lotions, soaps, and daily wear blocking the light rather than any structural fault. A professional clean and polish removes that residue, checks every stone is secure, and brings the sparkle straight back. If your ring is white gold, we can also re-apply the rhodium plating for that bright, \"like new\" finish. Bring it in and we'll tell you honestly whether it needs a clean or something more.</p>",
            "category": "Jewellery Repair & Remodelling"
          }
        },
        "q064": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can you repair jewellery I didn't buy from Griffin & Co?",
            "answer": "<p>Absolutely. We're happy to repair jewellery from anywhere, regardless of where it was originally purchased, and we treat every piece with the same care and attention as something we made ourselves. Whether it's an inherited heirloom, a high street find, or a piece from another jeweller, our goldsmiths will assess it properly and give you an honest quote. Visit our Chichester showroom, no appointment necessary for straightforward repairs, or get in touch beforehand for anything more complex.</p>",
            "category": "Jewellery Repair & Remodelling"
          }
        },
        "q065": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What is jewellery remodelling, and what can you do with my old pieces?",
            "answer": "<p>Remodelling takes jewellery you no longer wear, whether it's a single unworn ring or a drawer full of odds and ends, and transforms it into something new that you'll actually reach for. We can melt down and recast old gold or platinum, reset your existing diamonds and gemstones into a fresh design, combine elements from several pieces into one, or simply update an outdated setting to something more modern. Original metal and stones can often be carried through into the new piece, so the material history stays intact even as the design changes. It's a genuinely collaborative process, from your first consultation through sketches and CAD renders to the final polish in our Chichester workshop. Start the conversation on our [remodelling service](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/pages/jewellery-remodelling-service) page.</p>",
            "category": "Jewellery Repair & Remodelling"
          }
        },
        "q066": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How much does remodelling cost, and how long does it take?",
            "answer": "<p>Because every remodelling project starts from a different piece and a different vision, there's no fixed price list, and that's intentional; it reflects a proper, considered quote rather than a gap in our pricing. After a no-obligation consultation, where we assess your existing jewellery and talk through your ideas, we'll give you a detailed quote covering metal, gemstones, design complexity, and labour. Timelines vary in the same way, but you can generally expect the process to take anywhere from a few weeks to a couple of months from a finalised design to collection. We won't let your new piece leave the workshop until it meets our standards, however long that takes. Book a consultation through our [remodelling service](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/pages/jewellery-remodelling-service) page.</p>",
            "category": "Jewellery Repair & Remodelling"
          }
        },
        "q067": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Do you offer home visits for repairs and remodelling consultations?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes. For clients who value privacy or simply can't get to Chichester easily, our goldsmiths and designers can visit you at home across West Sussex, Surrey, Hampshire, and Greater London. We bring GIA-certified diamond samples, metal swatches, sizing tools, and design portfolios, so a repair assessment, valuation, or remodelling consultation can happen just as thoroughly in your own home as it would in our showroom. A typical home consultation lasts around 60 to 90 minutes, giving us proper time to talk through your piece without rushing. Request a visit through our [home visits](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/pages/home-visits) page.</p>",
            "category": "Jewellery Repair & Remodelling"
          }
        },
        "q068": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is there a fee for a home visit?",
            "answer": "<p>It depends on the nature of the visit. We offer complimentary initial consultations for bespoke and remodelling commissions within a 15-mile radius of Chichester, while repair collections and valuations may carry a small travel fee, which is deducted from the final cost of the work. Beyond that radius, or for more complex commissions, a consultation fee may apply depending on location and scope, and this too is typically credited toward your final quote. We'll always confirm any fee with you before the visit is booked, so there's never a surprise. Get in touch via our [home visits](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/pages/home-visits) page to check what applies to you.</p>",
            "category": "Jewellery Repair & Remodelling"
          }
        },
        "q069": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How do I find my bracelet or bangle size?",
            "answer": "<p>It depends on the style. For a flexible chain bracelet, wrap a tape measure or strip of paper just below your wrist bone, then add ease: 1 to 1.5cm for a snug fit, 2cm for a standard comfort fit, or 2.5cm if you prefer it loose. Bangles are rigid and don't open, so what matters is the widest part of your hand; bring your thumb across your palm to your pinky, then measure around your knuckles to find the minimum size that will slide on comfortably. Many of our chains can also be shortened or extended to your exact preference. See our [bracelet size guide](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/blogs/guide-jewellery-sizing-guide/bracelet-size-guide) for the full reference chart, or [get in touch](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/pages/schedule-an-appointment) for bespoke sizing advice.</p>",
            "category": "Jewellery Repair & Remodelling"
          }
        },
        "q070": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What necklace length should I choose?",
            "answer": "<p>It comes down to where you want the piece to sit. A 14 to 16 inch chain sits high as a choker, 18 inches is the classic \"princess\" length that rests on the collarbone and is the safest choice for a gift, 20 to 24 inches sits between the collarbone and bust for a versatile everyday or business look, and 30 inches or more drapes below the bust or works beautifully double-wrapped. If you're buying a pendant, remember the stone's weight will pull the chain into a slight \"V,\" so it will typically sit 1 to 2cm lower than a string test suggests. We can often source chains at custom lengths, so if you have a specific length in mind, just let us know. Read the full [necklace size guide](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/blogs/guide-jewellery-sizing-guide/necklace-size-guide) or [contact our team](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/pages/schedule-an-appointment) for advice.</p>",
            "category": "Jewellery Repair & Remodelling"
          }
        },
        "q071": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How do I find the right earring or hoop size?",
            "answer": "<p>For hoops and huggies, what matters is the inner diameter, not the outer size, since that's what determines the fit against your ear. Measure from your piercing hole to the bottom edge of your earlobe, then add around 1mm for a snug huggie fit or 2mm or more for a looser, classic hoop. For studs, 3 to 4mm reads as tiny and subtle, 5 to 6mm is the classic everyday size, and 7mm or above makes a genuine statement covering most of the lower lobe. High piercings or cartilage placements (helix, tragus) usually need smaller diameters, typically 6 to 8mm, so it's always worth measuring first rather than guessing. See our [earring size guide](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/blogs/guide-jewellery-sizing-guide/earring-size-guide) for the full reference chart.</p>",
            "category": "Jewellery Repair & Remodelling"
          }
        },
        "q072": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How should I clean my jewellery at home, and does it differ by metal?",
            "answer": "<p>For most gold, platinum, and diamond pieces, soak briefly (around 20 to 30 minutes) in warm water with a few drops of mild washing-up liquid, brush gently with a soft toothbrush around settings and claws, rinse thoroughly, and pat dry with a lint-free cloth. Rose gold needs prompt, thorough drying to avoid tarnishing, and sterling silver tarnishes through normal oxidation, so a dedicated silver polishing cloth used regularly is your best everyday tool. White gold should only ever be cleaned with warm water and a very soft cloth, since anything abrasive or acidic wears away the rhodium plating faster. Pearls, opals, emeralds, and other treated or porous stones are the exception to all of this: they should only ever be wiped with a soft, barely damp cloth, never soaked. Read our full [jewellery cleaning guide](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/blogs/jewellery-guides/jewellery-cleaner) for material-by-material detail.</p>",
            "category": "Jewellery Repair & Remodelling"
          }
        },
        "q073": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can I use an ultrasonic cleaner on my jewellery?",
            "answer": "<p>Only on the right pieces. Plain gold, platinum, and diamond jewellery without vulnerable stones generally tolerates ultrasonic cleaning well, but the vibrations are genuinely risky for anything set with pearls, opals, emeralds, or fracture-filled and treated gemstones, since they can dislodge fillings or damage delicate internal structure. If you're at all unsure what your piece contains, the safest approach is simply not to use one. Our GIA-certified specialists are always happy to check a piece for you before you clean it, whether you visit our Chichester showroom or book a private consultation. See our [jewellery cleaning guide](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/blogs/jewellery-guides/jewellery-cleaner) for more detail on which stones to avoid.</p>",
            "category": "Jewellery Repair & Remodelling"
          }
        },
        "q074": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How should I store my jewellery, and how do I stop silver from tarnishing?",
            "answer": "<p>Store each piece individually in a soft pouch or fabric-lined box, away from direct sunlight and humidity, so pieces don't scratch each other, tangle, or tarnish prematurely. Sterling silver needs a little extra attention, since it tarnishes through a natural reaction with sulphur in the air; storing it in an airtight bag with an anti-tarnish strip or silica gel packet slows this considerably, and a regular pass with a silver polishing cloth keeps everyday pieces bright between deeper cleans. If a piece has built up heavier tarnish or surface damage that a cloth can't shift, that's a job for a professional workshop rather than a stronger home product. Book an inspection through our [repair service](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/pages/jewellery-repair-service) page, or read our [jewellery cleaning guide](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/blogs/jewellery-guides/jewellery-cleaner) for more on caring for silver day to day.</p>",
            "category": "Jewellery Repair & Remodelling"
          }
        },
        "q075": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What is a lab-grown diamond?",
            "answer": "<p>A lab-grown diamond is a real diamond, created in a highly controlled laboratory rather than deep in the earth. Using advanced technology — HPHT (High Pressure, High Temperature) or CVD (Chemical Vapour Deposition) — scientists replicate the same conditions of extreme heat and pressure that form diamonds naturally. The result has the same carbon-atom structure as a mined stone, not a \"fake\" or a simulant like cubic zirconia. You can browse our [lab diamond jewellery](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/pages/lab-diamond-jewellery) to see the range for yourself.</p>",
            "category": "Lab-Grown Diamonds"
          }
        },
        "q076": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Are lab-grown diamonds \"real\" diamonds?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes, absolutely. A lab-grown diamond shares the exact same chemical composition, crystal structure, and optical properties as a diamond pulled from the earth — even an expert gemologist cannot tell the difference by eye. The only difference is origin: one formed over billions of years underground, the other in a controlled lab environment that speeds up the same natural process. We like to compare it to a wild orchid and a greenhouse-grown one — both are real, beautiful orchids.</p>",
            "category": "Lab-Grown Diamonds"
          }
        },
        "q077": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Are lab-grown diamonds fake, like cubic zirconia?",
            "answer": "<p>No — this is one of the most common misunderstandings we hear in the showroom. Cubic zirconia and moissanite are diamond simulants: different materials made to look like a diamond. A lab-grown diamond is not a look-alike, it is chemically and physically a diamond, with the same hardness and the same fire and brilliance as a mined stone. The distinction matters for durability and value, and it's why we're happy to talk you through it during a consultation.</p>",
            "category": "Lab-Grown Diamonds"
          }
        },
        "q078": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How are lab-grown diamonds actually made?",
            "answer": "<p>Lab-grown diamonds are grown from a small diamond \"seed\" using one of two processes. In HPHT, the seed is placed under extreme heat and pressure that mimics the earth's mantle. In CVD, carbon-rich gas is broken down inside a chamber so that carbon atoms settle onto the seed, layer by layer, until a full crystal has formed. Both methods take weeks rather than millennia, but the crystal that comes out the other end has grown atom by atom, just as a mined diamond did.</p>",
            "category": "Lab-Grown Diamonds"
          }
        },
        "q079": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How do lab-grown diamonds differ from mined diamonds?",
            "answer": "<p>The chemistry is identical — the difference is entirely in how each stone came to be. Mined diamonds form over billions of years deep within the earth's mantle before being brought to the surface. Lab-grown diamonds are created over a matter of weeks, in a laboratory, by growing carbon atoms onto a diamond seed under carefully controlled conditions. Once finished, the two are indistinguishable to the naked eye, and both are graded using the same standards.</p>",
            "category": "Lab-Grown Diamonds"
          }
        },
        "q080": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Are lab-grown diamonds graded the same way as mined diamonds?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes. Every lab-grown diamond we work with is independently graded and certified by the world's leading gemological labs — GIA and IGI — using exactly the same standard applied to mined stones: the 4 Cs of Cut, Colour, Clarity, and Carat. That certification means you can be completely confident in the quality of your stone before it ever reaches your ring. Read our [full guide to the 4 Cs of lab-grown diamonds](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/blogs/guide-lab-diamonds/the-griffin-co-guide-to-the-4-cs-of-lab-grown-diamonds) for more detail.</p>",
            "category": "Lab-Grown Diamonds"
          }
        },
        "q081": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What are the 4 Cs, and how do they apply to lab-grown diamonds?",
            "answer": "<p>Cut, Colour, Clarity, and Carat are the universal characteristics used to grade any diamond, mined or lab-grown. Cut measures the quality of a stone's facets and symmetry, and is the biggest factor in its sparkle. Colour is graded on a scale from colourless down to increasingly warm tones, Clarity measures how free a stone is from natural inclusions, and Carat is simply its weight. Because lab-grown diamonds are formed in such controlled conditions, it's often possible to find stones with exceptionally high colour and clarity grades — our team can talk you through the balance that suits your budget during a [private consultation](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/pages/schedule-an-appointment).</p>",
            "category": "Lab-Grown Diamonds"
          }
        },
        "q082": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Will a lab-grown diamond pass a diamond tester test?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes. Because a lab-grown diamond is structurally identical to a mined one, it will pass a traditional diamond tester every time — the tools jewellers use measure a stone's physical and thermal properties, and a lab-grown diamond has exactly the same ones. It's also graded by the same institutes, GIA and IGI, using the same 4 Cs criteria as mined stones. If you'd like to see this for yourself, we're always happy to show you during an in-showroom visit.</p>",
            "category": "Lab-Grown Diamonds"
          }
        },
        "q083": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How durable are lab-grown diamonds?",
            "answer": "<p>Just as durable as a mined diamond. Lab-grown diamonds score a 10 on the Mohs hardness scale — the same top score as their mined counterparts, and the hardest natural material there is. That means your lab-grown diamond will resist everyday scratches and wear for a lifetime, whether it's set in an engagement ring you wear daily or a pendant you save for special occasions.</p>",
            "category": "Lab-Grown Diamonds"
          }
        },
        "q084": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What are the main benefits of choosing a lab-grown diamond?",
            "answer": "<p>Two things tend to draw our clients towards lab-grown: value and traceability. Because the production process is more efficient with a shorter supply chain, a lab-grown diamond typically costs significantly less than a mined stone of the same size and quality — which often means you can choose a larger stone, or invest more into the setting and craftsmanship. On top of that, a lab-grown diamond's origin is 100% known and documented, giving you complete peace of mind about where it came from.</p>",
            "category": "Lab-Grown Diamonds"
          }
        },
        "q085": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How much less do lab-grown diamonds cost than mined diamonds?",
            "answer": "<p>As a guide, lab-grown diamonds typically cost around 20–40% less than a mined diamond of comparable size and quality. That difference comes down to a shorter, more efficient supply chain rather than any compromise on the stone itself — the diamond you end up with is graded to the same standard either way. For many of our clients, that gap is what makes a larger centre stone, or a more ambitious bespoke setting, possible within their budget.</p>",
            "category": "Lab-Grown Diamonds"
          }
        },
        "q086": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Are lab-grown diamonds an ethical choice?",
            "answer": "<p>For many of our clients, this is exactly why they choose lab-grown. A lab-grown diamond's origin is fully known and controlled from the moment it's created, which gives you an absolute guarantee that it carries no association with conflict — something that's much harder to trace with certainty through a long mined-diamond supply chain, even with safeguards like the Kimberley Process in place. It's a straightforward way to know the story behind your diamond is as good as the stone itself.</p>",
            "category": "Lab-Grown Diamonds"
          }
        },
        "q087": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What is the environmental impact of lab-grown diamonds compared to mined diamonds?",
            "answer": "<p>Traditional diamond mining is a resource-intensive process, involving the displacement of large volumes of earth and significant water and energy use. Producing a lab-grown diamond has a considerably lighter footprint by comparison, with lower water and land use than mining. It's one of the reasons we're proud to offer lab-grown stones alongside our mined and ethically sourced options — so you can choose what matters most to you without giving up on brilliance.</p>",
            "category": "Lab-Grown Diamonds"
          }
        },
        "q088": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Do lab-grown diamonds hold their resale value as well as mined diamonds?",
            "answer": "<p>This is worth knowing honestly: natural mined diamonds typically retain higher resale value than lab-grown stones. For most of our clients, that trade-off is worth it — the money saved on the stone itself goes towards a larger centre diamond or a more considered bespoke setting, and the ring is chosen to be worn and loved rather than resold. If resale value is a priority for you, it's a conversation worth having with our team before you choose your stone.</p>",
            "category": "Lab-Grown Diamonds"
          }
        },
        "q089": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Why are so many couples choosing lab-grown diamonds now?",
            "answer": "<p>Three reasons come up again and again in our showroom: value, ethics, and quality assurance. Lab-grown diamonds offer remarkable value for larger or higher-quality stones, they provide genuine peace of mind as a conflict-free choice, and they receive exactly the same independent grading from GIA and IGI as mined diamonds. Browse our [lab-grown diamond engagement rings](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/collections/lab-grown-diamond-engagement-rings) to see the styles our clients are choosing.</p>",
            "category": "Lab-Grown Diamonds"
          }
        },
        "q090": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can I design a bespoke engagement ring using a lab-grown diamond?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes, of course. Whether you work with our 3D online studio or book a private consultation at our Chichester showroom, you can choose from our full inventory of certified lab-grown diamonds as the centrepiece for your bespoke design. Our team will talk you through cut, colour, clarity, and carat as you go, so the stone you choose is one you fully understand and love. Start by [booking a private consultation](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/pages/schedule-an-appointment) with our design team.</p>",
            "category": "Lab-Grown Diamonds"
          }
        },
        "q091": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can I see and compare lab-grown diamonds in person before I decide?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes. The best way to appreciate a lab-grown diamond's brilliance is to see it in person, so we welcome you to our Chichester showroom for a relaxed, no-obligation consultation where you can compare cuts, colours, and carat weights side by side. If a showroom visit isn't easy for you, we also offer a complimentary home-visit concierge service, bringing a curated selection of loose lab diamonds, sizing tools, and design portfolios to you. Either way, you'll have everything you need to choose with confidence — [get in touch to arrange a visit](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/pages/schedule-an-appointment).</p>",
            "category": "Lab-Grown Diamonds"
          }
        },
        "q092": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Where can I learn more about choosing a lab-grown diamond?",
            "answer": "<p>We've put together a full set of guides covering everything from how lab-grown diamonds are made to how the 4 Cs affect your choice — you'll find them all in our [Lab-Grown Diamonds guide hub](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/blogs/guide-lab-diamonds), including our dedicated piece on [why lab-grown diamonds are the ethical choice](https://griffinfinejewellery.com/blogs/guide-lab-diamonds/why-lab-grown-diamonds-are-the-ethical-future-of-jewellery). If you'd rather talk it through with a person than read on your own, that's exactly what our consultations are for — no pressure, just honest guidance.</p>",
            "category": "Lab-Grown Diamonds"
          }
        }
      },
